    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-inverse-polynomials">
        <p>
            Consider the polynomial function <m>f(x)=2x+6</m>. In this activity, you will investigate how a function transforms an input into an output and explore how to reverse that process to recover the original input.
        </p>
        <p>
            Start by evaluating the function for each given value of <m>x</m> and complete the table.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>10</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Next, suppose you only know the output values of the function. Work backward to determine the input value <m>x</m> that produced each output and complete the table.
        </p>

        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>16</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>20</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            After completing both tables, discuss the questions below with a partner or as a group.
        </p>

        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                    Explain the steps you used to work backward from <m>f(x)</m> to determine <m>x</m>. In what order did you reverse the operations?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Let the output of the function be represented by <m>y</m>. Write an algebraic expression in terms of <m>y</m> that gives the original input <m>x</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Now consider the polynomial function <m>g(x)=x^2</m>. If the output is <m>25</m>, can you determine exactly one original input value? Explain your reasoning.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Based on your observations, what characteristics must a polynomial function have in order to possess an inverse function?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </activity>

