    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <p>
            To determine the inverse of a polynomial function algebraically, reverse the roles of the input and output values by following these steps.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Replace the function notation <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Interchange the variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. This reflects the fact that the inputs of the original function become the outputs of the inverse and the outputs become the inputs.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Solve the new equation for <m>y</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite the final expression using inverse notation <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>. The resulting expression represents the inverse function.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            This process only works when the original function is <em>one-to-one</em>, meaning that each output value corresponds to exactly one input value. Graphically, a one-to-one function passes the horizontal line test because no horizontal line intersects the graph more than once.
        </p>
        <p>
            A common example is a polynomial of the form <m>f(x)=ax^n+c</m>, where <m>a\neq0</m> and <m>n</m> is an odd positive integer. Functions of this form are always one-to-one because they increase or decrease continuously without repeating output values. Their inverse can be found algebraically as
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
            f^{-1}(x)=\sqrt[n]{\frac{x-c}{a}}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            For example, if <m>f(x)=2x^3+5</m>, then its inverse is
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
            f^{-1}(x)=\sqrt[3]{\frac{x-5}{2}}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            In contrast, polynomial functions with even powers such as <m>f(x)=x^2</m> are not one-to-one over all real numbers because different inputs can produce the same output. For instance,
            <m>f(2)=4</m> and <m>f(-2)=4</m>. Such functions do not have inverses unless their domains are restricted.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-linear">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the inverse function of the polynomial function <m>f(x) = 5x - 4</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>y = 5x - 4</md></p>
            <p>
                Switch <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> to get the inverse equation.
            </p>
            <p><md>x = 5y - 4</md></p>
            <p>
                Solve the equation for <m>y</m> by isolating the variable.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                <mrow>x + 4 \amp = 5y</mrow>
                <mrow>\frac{x + 4}{5} \amp = y</mrow>
                <mrow>\frac{1}{5}x + \frac{4}{5} \amp = y</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p> 
                Since <m>f(x)</m> is a linear function with a non-zero slope, it is one-to-one. We can write the final inverse function using standard function notation.
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{1}{5}x + \frac{4}{5}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-cubic">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the inverse for the function <m>f(x) = x^3 + 2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Write the function as an equation with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> variables.
            </p>
            <p><md>y = x^3 + 2</md></p>
            <p>
                Switch the input and the output variables to begin finding the inverse.
            </p>
            <p><md>x = y^3 + 2</md></p>
            <p>
                Subtract 2 from both sides, and then take the cube root of both sides to fully isolate <m>y</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>
                <mrow>x - 2 \amp = y^3</mrow>
                <mrow>\sqrt[3]{x - 2} \amp = y</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because <m>f(x) = x^3 + 2</m> is a one-to-one function, its inverse is also a function, allowing us to use the <m>f^{-1}</m> notation.
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[3]{x - 2}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-odd-power">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the inverse function of <m>f(x) = 4x^5 + 7</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We can approach this by exchanging <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> and solving, or by relying on the general formula for odd polynomials. Using the formula for <m>f(x) = ax^n + c</m> when <m>n</m> is odd, we identify the key parameters. 
            </p>
            <p>
                We identify <m>a = 4</m>, <m>c = 7</m>, and the degree <m>n = 5</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Substituting these directly into the general form <m>f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[n]{\frac{1}{a}(x-c)}</m> gives us the inverse immediately.
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[5]{\frac{1}{4}(x - 7)}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-even-power">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the inverse function of <m>h(x) = 2x^8 + 5</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We inspect the degree of the polynomial to determine its properties. By identifying <m>a = 2</m>, <m>c = 5</m>, and <m>n = 8</m>, we see that the exponent <m>n</m> is an even number.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>n = 8</m> is even, not odd, the function <m>h(x)</m> is not one-to-one and does not have an inverse function.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

